@implements IDisposable
@inject AccountCharacterStore Store
@inject CharacterSession Session
@inject Accounts Accounts
@inject DiscardedCharacter Discarded
@inject ApiMembershipStore Memberships
@inject AccountCampaignStore Campaigns
@inject AlternateFormEngine Forms

@* The characters this browser — or this account — is holding, and the way between them.

   **It replaced a panel called "Starting over", which was the first thing on the page.** That
   panel offered one destructive button and a link away to somebody else's finished character, which
   is a strange greeting for somebody who came here to build one. What belongs at the top is what
   you already have.

   **Starting another character and importing one keep the character on screen, and that is new.**
   Both used to write over it — "Start a new character" emptied the slot it was in, and an import
   overwrote whatever the current-character pointer was aimed at — so this browser could hold
   exactly one character however many the list was built to draw. Both now go through
   `AccountCharacterStore.StartAnotherAsync`, which writes the character down under its own id and
   moves the pointer before anything here touches the session.

   The ordering property that came across from the old panel still holds and still matters, only
   the other way up: **the keep lands before the session is emptied.** Setting the session off
   raises its change event, which starts a fire-and-forget write; emptying first races that write
   against the move and can put an empty sheet over the character being kept. `StartAgainTests`
   asserts the interop ordering, not just the outcome.

   **Discarding no longer asks first.** It used to open a confirm in the page whenever there was
   something to lose — the same two-step dialogue a browser `confirm()` is, only quieter. It now
   acts the moment it is clicked and leaves a single-level undo behind it instead, offered from
   the banner: see `CharacterSession.StartAgain`, `.ReplaceWithUndo` and `UndoTests`. A confirm
   pays its cost on every use; undo only pays on the uses that turn out to be mistakes. It is the
   one destructive control left on this panel.

   ── The layout, redesigned rather than adjusted ─────────────────────────────────────────────

   **The panel holds one kind of thing and offers four actions on it, and its old layout encoded
   neither.** Every row was a name at the left edge and two buttons at the right, across a gap that
   grew with the window; the commonest action — opening a character — was the smallest thing on the
   row and the furthest away, while the rarest and most reversible one, discarding, was the only
   thing drawn in danger red. Beneath them "Start a new character" and "Import a character" floated
   unequal and uncontained, though both do the same job. Three faults, three moves:

   * **The row is the control.** A character's name is a real, full-width button that opens it —
     the same idiom a Trait row's name already uses. Discard shrinks to a quiet trailing button.
     The large easy target is the safe act and the small distant one is the destructive act, which
     is the way round Fitts's law should be pointed.
   * **The open character has its own block, above the list.** The panel could not say which
     character you were in: one reaches the list only once it is worth keeping, so straight after
     *Start a new character* the list showed the one you kept and nothing marked open at all. This
     block always names the character on screen and carries its spend.
   * **The two ways to make a character are one bar.** Equal weight, under a rule. The group's own
     edge is what separates them from the list, which is the job `ImportCharacter`'s `.small` was
     doing alone and badly.

   **A row carries a time and never a cost, and that is a fact about the data rather than a
   preference.** `SavedCharacters` keeps an index of labels and timestamps and holds each
   character's payload under its own key — its remarks say a list "should not have to deserialize
   and cost every one of them just to print a label and a timestamp". So `UpdatedAt` is free and a
   Hero Point figure is a read, a cost and a validate per row. The open character is the one
   exception, because the session is already holding it.

   **The count is stated once, in `Aside`, not repeated in the closing sentence too.** It used to
   be both — "3 of 5" beside the title and "3 of 5 on this account" again at the foot — which is
   the same fact read twice on a panel with four other things competing for the same glance. The
   sentence at the foot now says only what `Aside` cannot: where a character actually lives.

   ── Two shapes, because a roster of thirty is not a panel on step one ────────────────────────

   **The list moved to `/characters` — its own area now, not a page under `/build` — and this
   panel is drawn in both places.** At twenty-nine characters it was the tallest thing on the tier
   page, so somebody who came to choose a tier scrolled a screen of other people's characters to
   reach the six cards — and a GM sorting NPCs is not choosing a tier at all. `ListsEveryCharacter`
   is what differs: the tier page keeps the character on screen and the two ways to make one, and
   links to the rest; the roster page draws the list itself.

   **One component and not two, because the actions are the same actions.** Starting another
   character and importing one both go through `Keep`, which reports a refusal in words — a second
   copy of that would be a second chance for one of them to forget to say anything. What the
   parameter turns off is a list, not a behaviour.

   **The filter is `OptionFilter.Matches`, which is the rule the six pick-lists and the command
   palette already share.** A reader who has learnt that "plast" finds Plasticity has learnt
   something about this app, and a roster that matched differently would be teaching them it was
   about one list. The box is `.options-filter` for the same reason — the idiom exists.

   **Grouped by game, and the heading only appears when there is more than one group.** The
   campaign is already on every row (`SavedCharacterSummary.CampaignId`, free) so this costs no
   read; but an account whose characters are all in the same place has nothing to tell apart, and
   a single heading reading "In no game" over every row would be answering a question nobody
   asked. With one group the sentence that was always there — where these characters live — is
   the heading instead.

   **Nothing here is drawn below `ToolsFrom` rows.** A search box, an order control and group
   headings over four characters are furniture on a list a reader can already see the whole of.
   Same rule as `_showTimes`, one threshold up. *@

<Panel Title="Your characters" Aside="@Aside" NoPrint="true">
    <ChildContent>

    @if (_characters.Count == 0 && !Session.HasSomethingToLose)
    {
        @* **Says what to do, and where the character will actually be found**, rather than only
           that there is nothing yet — the same rule every other empty list in this app follows.
           The two branches used to be one sentence claiming "remembered in this browser" whoever
           was reading it, which was simply false for a signed-in account.

           **The open-character block is deliberately not drawn here**: on a first visit it would be
           a frame around a name nobody has chosen, and what a newcomer needs is the sentence
           telling them where to start. It appears the moment there is anything to be open. *@
        <EmptyState>
            @if (_limit is not null)
            {
                <text>Nothing built yet. Start one below — it is kept on your account.</text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>Nothing built yet. Start one below — it is kept in this browser.</text>
            }
        </EmptyState>
    }
    else
    {
        @* The character on screen, named whether or not it has reached the list yet.

           **The name comes from the session, not from the index.** It is live on the finishing
           step where somebody is typing it, and a label read out of a list refreshed on save would
           lag by a keystroke — the same reason the banner's switcher reads the session. *@
        <div class="character-open">
            <span class="who">
                <span class="lbl">Open now</span>
                <b class="nm">@OpenName</b>
                <span class="meta">@Standing</span>
                @* **Which sheet counts at the table**, which is the question this answers and the
                   spend beside it does not. Drawn only for a character that is actually in a game:
                   "Not submitted" over one nobody has put in a campaign would be an answer to a
                   question the reader has not asked. *@
                @if (CampaignStandingFor(_currentId) is { } standing)
                {
                    <span class="meta">@standing</span>
                }
            </span>

            @* Only when there is something to throw away. An empty slot has nothing to discard,
               and a button that would do nothing is worse than no button. *@
            @if (Session.HasSomethingToLose)
            {
                @* The same character, read as a document — no steps, no budget strip, the shape
                   that goes to a printer or sits on the table during play. Gated the same way as
                   Discard beside it and for the same reason: an empty slot's sheet is a page of
                   blank rules, which is the published form and still not worth offering. *@
                <a class="btn small quiet" href="sheet">Sheet</a>
                <button type="button" class="btn small quiet" @onclick="DiscardOpen">Discard</button>
            }
        </div>

        @if (_others.Count > 0 && !ListsEveryCharacter)
        {
            @* **The tier page says what it has and where the rest are, and stops.** Everything
               below this — the filter, the order, the group headings and the rows themselves —
               belongs to the roster page, which is the whole of the move: this page is about
               tiers. The count is here because "all your characters" with no number is a door
               onto an unknown quantity, and knowing there are twenty-eight is most of the reason
               to walk through it. *@
            <p class="others-head">@Elsewhere</p>
            <p><a class="btn" href="characters">All your characters (@_others.Count)</a></p>
        }
        else if (_others.Count > 0)
        {
            @if (_others.Count >= ToolsFrom)
            {
                @* The pick-lists' own box, class and all. `role="searchbox"` is not set and no
                   combobox pattern is claimed: this filter drives no cursor and owns no listbox,
                   unlike `OptionList`'s, so announcing one would promise a keyboard behaviour that
                   is not here. A plain labelled search field is what this is. *@
                <div class="options-filter">
                    <input type="search" value="@_query" @oninput="Typed"
                           placeholder="Find a character or a game"
                           aria-label="Find a character or a game" />
                    <span class="options-count">@Shown of @_others.Count</span>
                </div>

                @* Three orders, and the reader's choice is not written down anywhere. It is a
                   fact about this glance at this list, not a preference about the app — the same
                   reason the open-character pointer is per-browser and not per-account. *@
                <div class="roster-order" role="group" aria-label="Order these characters">
                    @foreach (var order in Orders)
                    {
                        <button type="button"
                                aria-pressed="@(_order == order ? "true" : "false")"
                                @onclick="() => Reorder(order)">@Word(order)</button>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            @foreach (var group in Grouped())
            {
                <p class="others-head">
                    @group.Heading
                    @if (group.Aside is { } aside)
                    {
                        <span class="options-count">@aside</span>
                    }
                </p>

                <ul class="character-list">
                    @foreach (var row in Arrange(group.Characters))
                    {
                        var one = row.Row;
                        <li class="@(row.Indent ? "variant-child" : "")">
                        @* **The row is the button.** Opening is what a reader came here to do, and
                           it is now the whole width of the row rather than a chip at the far edge.
                           A real `<button>`, never a `<div>` with a click handler — the rule every
                           control in this app follows. *@
                        <button type="button" class="open-target" @onclick="() => Open(one.Id)">
                            <span class="nm">@one.Label</span>

                            @* **Only where the list actually holds both, which is the same
                               argument the time makes.** On a roster that is all Villains the chip
                               is on every row and tells a reader nothing; on a GM's, where the
                               party and the opposition are in one list, it is the fastest thing to
                               read on the row. A kind this app does not write draws nothing rather
                               than a guess — see `Kinds.Word`. *@
                            @if (_showKinds && Kinds.Word(one.Kind) is { } kind)
                            {
                                <span class="kind">@kind</span>
                            }

                            @if (CampaignStandingFor(one.Id) is { } inAGame)
                            {
                                <span class="meta">@inAGame</span>
                            }

                            @* **The figure a row could not carry until the index held it.** It is
                               not costed here: `SavedCharacters` wrote it when the character was
                               saved, which is what keeps a list of thirty from being thirty reads.
                               A character the engine declined to price shows its tier and no
                               number, which is the front door's rule and not a gap. *@
                            @if (Says(one) is { } what)
                            {
                                <span class="meta">@what</span>
                            }

                            @* **The time now earns its place only when it explains the order.**
                               It was the one thing telling two rows apart, which is why it was
                               drawn from two characters up; on a list of twenty-nine imported in
                               one sitting it read "3 minutes ago" on every row, and the fields
                               beside it say far more. Under "Recent" it is what the order *means*,
                               so it stays there. *@
                            @* `.when` names what this phrase is, so a test can ask for the time
                               rather than sniffing the row's text for the word "ago" — which
                               `Ages` does not always use ("just now") and which the spend beside
                               it could one day contain. `.meta` still carries the styling. *@
                            @if (ShowsTime && Ages.Since(one.UpdatedAt, DateTimeOffset.UtcNow) is { } age)
                            {
                                <span class="meta when">@age</span>
                            }
                        </button>

                        @* **Item 21: what this row's link reads as, or the browser-side finding
                           when the root it names is not held anywhere — the same shape
                           `UNKNOWN_CAMPAIGN` takes, computed here because the engine cannot see
                           the roster.** *@
                        @if (row.Note is { } note)
                        {
                            <span class="meta @(row.Warning ? "warning" : "")">
                                @(row.Warning ? $"Warning: {note}" : note)
                            </span>
                        }

                        @* Item 21's Trait Cap ruling and the rest of Ch.2 p.21, surfaced beside a
                           member's own row exactly as VARIANT_ROOT_NOT_HELD is above — computed
                           by AlternateFormRosterNotes, which is the only place this component
                           calls the engine about a family rather than about one sheet. *@
                        @foreach (var extra in FamilyNotesFor(one.Id))
                        {
                            <span class="meta warning">
                                @(extra.Warning ? $"Warning: {extra.Text}" : $"Error: {extra.Text}")
                            </span>
                        }
                        @if (FamilyPoolFor(one.Id) is { } pool)
                        {
                            <span class="meta">@pool</span>
                        }

                        @* No question first — see the comment on the panel above. Both kinds of
                           row are undoable from the banner the moment this fires: the one on
                           screen through the character's own buffer, any other row through
                           `DiscardedCharacter`, which reads it back before deleting it and can
                           write it to the same id again.

                           **Quiet, not danger red, and that reverses a deliberate choice.** The
                           red was picked so a destructive control looked as serious as what it
                           does — but the confirm was removed *because* undo makes discarding
                           cheap, and red repeated on every row made the loudest thing in the panel
                           its rarest and most reversible action. Red is kept for what cannot be
                           undone. *@
                        @* **Read this one without opening it**, which is the whole reason the
                           address takes an id. The row's own button switches the app to this
                           character; this link does not touch the current-character pointer, so
                           looking at an old one cannot make the next autosave write over what is
                           actually open. See `SheetPage`. *@
                        <a class="btn small quiet" href="sheet/@one.Id">Sheet</a>

                        <button type="button" class="btn small quiet"
                                @onclick="() => ToggleVersionOf(one.Id)">
                            Version of…
                        </button>

                        <button type="button" class="btn small quiet" @onclick="() => Delete(one.Id)">
                            Discard
                        </button>

                        @* **The chooser: another held character and a kind, or "Not a version" to
                           clear it.** It excludes this row itself and anything
                           `CharacterVariants.WouldCreateCycle` refuses, because the engine cannot
                           see the roster and so cannot refuse a cycle on its own — see that
                           method's remarks. *@
                        @if (_versionOfOpenId == one.Id)
                        {
                            <div class="version-of-chooser">
                                <Field Label="Version of" For="@($"version-of-root-{one.Id}")">
                                    <select id="@($"version-of-root-{one.Id}")" value="@_versionOfRoot"
                                            @onchange="@((ChangeEventArgs e) => _versionOfRoot = e.Value?.ToString() ?? "")">
                                        <option value="">Not a version of another character</option>
                                        @foreach (var candidate in RootCandidatesFor(one.Id))
                                        {
                                            <option value="@candidate.Id">@candidate.Label</option>
                                        }
                                    </select>
                                </Field>

                                @if (_versionOfRoot.Length > 0)
                                {
                                    <Field Label="Kind" For="@($"version-of-kind-{one.Id}")">
                                        <select id="@($"version-of-kind-{one.Id}")" value="@_versionOfKind"
                                                @onchange="ChangeVersionOfKind">
                                            @foreach (var kind in VersionKinds)
                                            {
                                                <option value="@kind">@Capitalise(CharacterVariants.Label(kind))</option>
                                            }
                                        </select>
                                    </Field>
                                }

                                <button type="button" class="btn small" @onclick="() => SaveVersionOf(one.Id)">
                                    Save
                                </button>
                                <button type="button" class="btn small quiet"
                                        @onclick="() => ClearVersionOf(one.Id)">
                                    Not a version
                                </button>
                            </div>
                        }
                        </li>
                    }
                </ul>
            }

            @* Only when a filter is the reason it is empty — the same rule `OptionList` follows.
               A roster that is empty because there is genuinely nothing in it is the branch
               above, which says where to start instead. *@
            @if (_query.Length > 0 && Shown == 0)
            {
                <p class="options-empty">No character or game matches what you typed.</p>
            }
        }
    }

    @* **A Villain handed to a campaign does not vanish from the list that held it** — the owner's
       ruling: the row says it was given as a nemesis. Its own row is gone from the account, so it
       is drawn from the memberships, and it opens nothing: the sheet is the GM's now. The same
       rule an unreadable row follows elsewhere, that a reader is told which state a thing is in.
       Only on the roster page, where every row is; the tier page lists none of them. *@
    @if (ListsEveryCharacter && GivenAway.Count > 0)
    {
        <p class="others-head">Given away</p>
        <ul class="character-list">
            @foreach (var one in GivenAway)
            {
                <li class="given-away">
                    <span class="nm">@one.Label</span>
                    <span class="meta">@Standings.Say(one.Standing, one.GivenTo)</span>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    }

    @* One bar, two equal doors. Starting fresh and importing something made elsewhere are the same
       kind of act — both produce a character that does not exist yet — so neither is demoted by
       size. The rule above the group is what separates them from the list, which is the job
       `ImportCharacter`'s `.small` used to do by being visibly smaller than its neighbour. *@
    <div class="make-another">
        @* "Start a new character" keeps its exact wording. It is what the old panel called this and
           what AreaTests asserts the tier page offers — and the act is the same act, so renaming it
           would cost a reader the only continuity they have.

           **No question first, and now nothing to ask about.** It used to empty the slot the
           character was in, so it was the most destructive control on the page and was drawn in
           the danger colour to say so. It now keeps that character under its own id and opens a
           fresh slot beside it — see `AccountCharacterStore.StartAnotherAsync`. *@
        <button type="button" class="btn primary" @onclick="StartNew">Start a new character</button>

        <ImportCharacter OnImported="Imported" />
    </div>

    @* **A refused click has to say so, or it reads as a broken button.** Starting another
       character and importing one both keep what is on screen rather than writing over it, so
       when there is nowhere to put the new one they do nothing at all — and doing nothing is
       indistinguishable from a control that is not wired up. `role="status"` because this appears
       in response to a click and is the only report of it.

       It takes the place of the standing "account is full" line rather than sitting beside it:
       that line already says the same thing, and two sentences about the same cap under one
       button is the fact-read-twice this panel's own comment warns about. *@
    @if (_refusal is not null)
    {
        <p class="small muted spaced" role="status">@_refusal</p>
    }
    else if (_full)
    {
        <p class="small muted spaced" role="status">
            Your account is full. Discard one to make room; this one is still saved.
        </p>
    }

    <p class="small muted spaced">
        @if (_limit is not null)
        {
            <text>They follow you to any browser you sign in to.</text>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>These are in this browser only. Sign in and they follow you instead.</text>
        }
    </p>

    </ChildContent>
</Panel>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this is the roster or the summary the tier page carries.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It turns off a list, never a behaviour.</b> Both shapes name the character on
    /// screen, both offer the two ways to make one, and both report a refusal in the same words —
    /// see <see cref="Keep"/>. What the roster adds is the other characters, the box to find one
    /// with and the headings that group them; what the tier page shows instead is how many there
    /// are and the way to them.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Defaulted to the roster because that is what this component was before there were
    /// two of them, so a caller that says nothing gets what every existing caller got.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ListsEveryCharacter { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// The Villains this account handed to a campaign, which have no row of their own any more.
    /// Empty — never null — where the memberships could not be read, because a list of things
    /// given away that failed to load is a list that says nothing, not one that says "none".
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<MembershipSummary> GivenAway =>
        _memberships?.Where(m => m.HandedOver).ToList() ?? [];

    /// <summary>
    /// How many other characters there have to be before this panel draws a filter box, an order
    /// control and group headings.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The same argument <c>_showTimes</c> makes, one threshold up.</b> A time earns its
    /// place once there are two characters to tell apart; a search box, three order buttons and a
    /// set of headings earn theirs only once the list is longer than a glance. Below this the
    /// panel is exactly what it was — a heading and some rows — because that is the right answer
    /// for the account that has four.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private const int ToolsFrom = 6;

    private IReadOnlyList<SavedCharacterSummary> _characters = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<SavedCharacterSummary> _others = [];
    private string? _currentId;

    /// <summary>
    /// Item 21's Alternate Form findings, keyed by the member and by the root — see
    /// <see cref="AlternateFormRosterNotes"/>. Recomputed every <see cref="Refresh"/>, the same
    /// as every other whole-account read this panel makes, and empty for an account that holds
    /// no alternate_form family at all, which costs nothing beyond the grouping <c>Arrange</c>
    /// already does.
    /// </summary>
    private AlternateFormRosterNotes.Result _formNotes = AlternateFormRosterNotes.Result.Empty;

    /// <summary>What the reader has typed into the filter box. Empty admits everything.</summary>
    private string _query = "";

    /// <summary>Which order the rows are in. Not persisted — see the control's own comment.</summary>
    private RosterOrder _order = RosterOrder.ByGame;

    /// <summary>
    /// What each of this account's campaigns is called, by id.
    ///
    /// <para><b>One call for the whole panel, like the standings beside it.</b> A group heading
    /// needs a name and a row carries only an id, so the alternative is a resolve per row — which
    /// is the read-per-row this panel's own remarks refuse for the Hero Point figure.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Empty is not "no campaigns", it is "none known here".</b> A signed-out visitor and
    /// a laptop with no network both land here, and an id this map cannot name is drawn under a
    /// heading that says so rather than under one invented for it.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private RosterNames _names = RosterNames.None;

    /// <summary>
    /// Where each of this account's characters stands in the games it is in, or null when that
    /// could not be asked.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Null is not an empty list.</b> A signed-out visitor, or a laptop with no network,
    /// gets no standing at all rather than "Not submitted" on every row — which would tell
    /// somebody their character is out of a game it is still in.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>One call for the whole panel, not one per row.</b> A membership carries no payload,
    /// so this is a single list, unlike the Hero Point figure a row deliberately does not
    /// carry — see the panel's own remarks on why every other row shows a time instead.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<MembershipSummary>? _memberships;

    private int? _limit;
    private bool _full;
    private bool _showTimes;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a row draws which palette its character is built in.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Only where the list holds both, which is the same rule the time follows.</b> A GM's
    /// roster mixes the party and the opposition and the chip is the fastest thing on the row; a
    /// player's is all Heroes, where it would be one word repeated down the whole list. A row whose
    /// kind was never written counts as neither, so an index that predates the field does not turn
    /// the chip on for a list that has nothing to tell apart.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool _showKinds;

    /// <summary>
    /// What to say about the last refused click, or null when nothing has been refused. Cleared at
    /// the start of every attempt, so it never describes an act two clicks ago.
    /// </summary>
    private string? _refusal;

    /// <summary>
    /// Item 21: the id of the row whose "Version of…" chooser is open, or null when none is.
    /// One at a time — opening a second closes the first, the same idiom a disclosure anywhere
    /// else in this app follows.
    /// </summary>
    private string? _versionOfOpenId;

    /// <summary>The chooser's own selections, reset every time it opens on a different row.</summary>
    private string _versionOfRoot = "";

    private string _versionOfKind = CharacterVariant.Later;

    /// <summary>
    /// The kinds the chooser's second box offers, read here rather than named as
    /// <c>CharacterVariant.Kinds</c> in the markup — <c>NoPageNamesATypeThisProjectDeclares</c>
    /// scans the templated region of every page for a compound type name printed at a player, and
    /// a component naming its own engine types on screen is exactly what that guard exists to
    /// catch.
    /// </summary>
    private static IReadOnlyList<string> VersionKinds { get; } = CharacterVariant.Kinds;

    private void ChangeVersionOfKind(ChangeEventArgs e) =>
        _versionOfKind = e.Value?.ToString() is { Length: > 0 } chosen ? chosen : CharacterVariant.Later;

    private string Aside => _limit is { } limit ? $"{_characters.Count} of {limit}" : "In this browser";

    /// <summary>
    /// Where the characters that are not on screen actually live.
    ///
    /// <para>The sentence this panel has always carried over its list, and it is now also the
    /// heading a single group falls back to — see <see cref="Roster.Group"/>. One spelling,
    /// because two would drift and the tier page prints it as well.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string Elsewhere => _limit is not null ? "Also on this account" : "Also in this browser";

    /// <summary>The three orders, in the order the control offers them.</summary>
    private static readonly RosterOrder[] Orders =
        [RosterOrder.ByGame, RosterOrder.Alphabetical, RosterOrder.Recent];

    /// <summary>What one order's button reads.</summary>
    private static string Word(RosterOrder order) => order switch
    {
        RosterOrder.ByGame => "By game",
        RosterOrder.Alphabetical => "A–Z",
        _ => "Recent",
    };

    /// <summary>How many rows the filter is letting through, across every group.</summary>
    private int Shown => _others.Count(c => Roster.Admits(c, _query, _names));

    /// <summary>The rows to draw, in their groups. Recomputed per render; see <see cref="Roster"/>.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<RosterGroup> Grouped() =>
        Roster.Group(_others, _query, _order, _names, Elsewhere);

    private void Typed(ChangeEventArgs e) => _query = e.Value?.ToString() ?? "";

    private void Reorder(RosterOrder order) => _order = order;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a row draws the time it was last touched.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It used to be drawn whenever there were two characters, and that was right while
    /// it was the only thing telling two rows apart.</b> A row now says which game, which kind and
    /// what it cost, and on twenty-nine characters imported in one sitting the time read
    /// "3 minutes ago" on every one of them — the column that was there to disambiguate had
    /// stopped doing it.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>So it is kept in the two places it still says something: on a list too short to have
    /// an order control, where it is what it always was, and under <c>Recent</c>, where it is what
    /// the chosen order <em>means</em>.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool ShowsTime =>
        _showTimes && (_others.Count < ToolsFrom || _order == RosterOrder.Recent);

    /// <summary>
    /// What one row says about itself besides its name — the spend and the tier, or as much of
    /// them as is known.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Null rather than a placeholder when neither is known</b>, which is every row
    /// written before the index carried them and every one whose next save has not happened yet.
    /// A row that said "— HP · —" would be reporting the index's age as though it were a fact
    /// about the character.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>A tier id that the rules do not know is printed as itself</b>, the same choice
    /// <c>CampaignDiff</c> makes: a payload can name a tier these rules have not got, and a row
    /// reading "Unnamed" tells a GM nothing while the id at least tells them what to look up.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? Says(SavedCharacterSummary one)
    {
        var tier = one.TierId is null
            ? null
            : _names.Tiers.GetValueOrDefault(one.TierId, one.TierId);

        return (one.Spent, tier) switch
        {
            (null, null) => null,
            (null, { } only) => only,
            ({ } hp, null) => $"{hp} HP",
            ({ } hp, { } named) => $"{hp} HP · {named}",
        };
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What to say about the games one character is in, or null when there is nothing to say.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Null for three different reasons, and all three are right to be silent.</b> The
    /// standings could not be read; this character is in no campaign; or there is no id to match
    /// against because the open slot is empty. In every one of them a printed standing would be an
    /// answer to a question nobody asked.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>A character in two games says both.</b> The question is "which sheet do I print at
    /// the table", and one campaign's answer is not the other's.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Neither is named, because nothing here can name one.</b> This passed the
    /// character's own label as the campaign's for as long as it existed, which rendered the same
    /// name against every row and told a reader nothing. A membership carries a campaign id and no
    /// label, and a player belongs to games they do not own, so there is no second read that would
    /// answer it — the label would have to come off the wire. Until it does, two standings side by
    /// side say what each is without claiming which game each is about.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? CampaignStandingFor(string? characterId)
    {
        if (characterId is null || _memberships is null) return null;

        var mine = _memberships.Where(m => m.CharacterId == characterId).ToList();
        if (mine.Count == 0) return null;

        // **`m.Label` is the CHARACTER's name, not the game's, and passing it here said the
        // wrong thing in the reader's own words.** `Standings.Say`'s second argument is what the
        // campaign is called; a character in two games rendered "Approved for Ninefold · Changes
        // pending" — the same name twice, disambiguating nothing, on a screen whose whole reason
        // for naming the campaign is that one game's answer is not the other's.
        //
        // **Nothing here can name the game**, and that is the real gap: a `MembershipSummary`
        // carries `CampaignId` and no label, and a player is a member of campaigns they do not
        // own, so `ApiCampaignStore` cannot look one up either. So this says the standing and
        // claims nothing — see PROGRESS.md, which records what putting the label on the wire
        // would take.

        return string.Join(" · ", mine.Select(m => Standings.Say(m.Standing)));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Item 21: <paramref name="characters"/> — one campaign heading's rows — re-ordered into
    /// root-then-children order, each carrying what to print beside it.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Built local to this heading, then corrected against the whole account.</b>
    /// <see cref="CharacterVariants.Group"/> only sees the rows it is handed, so a child whose
    /// root is in a <em>different</em> game — or is the character open above this panel, which is
    /// never in any heading's list at all — reads as an orphan from here alone. That is corrected
    /// below: a root missing from this heading but present anywhere in <see cref="_characters"/>
    /// is not drawn (there is nowhere to put its row in this heading) but is not warned about
    /// either, and the child still says what kind of version it is. Only a root this account does
    /// not hold at all earns <see cref="CharacterVariants.RootNotHeldCode"/>.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<(SavedCharacterSummary Row, bool Indent, string? Note, bool Warning)>
        Arrange(IReadOnlyList<SavedCharacterSummary> characters)
    {
        var byId = characters.ToDictionary(c => c.Id, StringComparer.Ordinal);
        var heldAnywhere = _characters.Select(c => c.Id).ToHashSet(StringComparer.Ordinal);
        var families = CharacterVariants.Group(characters.Select(CharacterVariants.FromSummary));

        var result = new List<(SavedCharacterSummary, bool, string?, bool)>();

        foreach (var family in families)
        {
            if (family.Root is { } root)
            {
                result.Add((byId[root.Id], false, null, false));

                foreach (var child in family.Children)
                    result.Add((byId[child.Id], true, CharacterVariants.Label(child.Variant!.Kind), false));

                continue;
            }

            foreach (var child in family.Children)
            {
                var namesA = child.Variant!.OfCharacterId;
                var heldElsewhere = heldAnywhere.Contains(namesA);

                result.Add((
                    byId[child.Id], false,
                    heldElsewhere ? CharacterVariants.Label(child.Variant.Kind)
                                  : CharacterVariants.RootNotHeldMessage(child),
                    !heldElsewhere));
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Every character this row could honestly be recorded as a version of: everything held,
    /// minus itself and minus anything <see cref="CharacterVariants.WouldCreateCycle"/> refuses —
    /// the engine cannot see the roster and so cannot refuse a cycle on its own, which is why this
    /// is asked here rather than left to <c>UNKNOWN_VARIANT_KIND</c>'s neighbours.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<SavedCharacterSummary> RootCandidatesFor(string characterId)
    {
        var lookup = _characters.ToDictionary(
            c => c.Id, c => c.VariantOf, StringComparer.Ordinal);

        return
        [
            .. _characters
                .Where(c => c.Id != characterId)
                .Where(c => !CharacterVariants.WouldCreateCycle(characterId, c.Id, lookup))
                .OrderBy(c => c.Label, StringComparer.CurrentCulture)
        ];
    }

    private static string Capitalise(string? word) =>
        string.IsNullOrEmpty(word) ? "" : char.ToUpperInvariant(word[0]) + word[1..];

    /// <summary>
    /// Opens the chooser on one row, closing whichever was open before. Seeded from that row's
    /// own current link, so reopening it shows what it already says rather than a blank form.
    /// </summary>
    private void ToggleVersionOf(string characterId)
    {
        if (_versionOfOpenId == characterId)
        {
            _versionOfOpenId = null;
            return;
        }

        _versionOfOpenId = characterId;

        var current = _characters.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Id == characterId);
        _versionOfRoot = current?.VariantOf ?? "";
        _versionOfKind = current?.VariantKind is { Length: > 0 } kind ? kind : CharacterVariant.Later;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Writes the chooser's choice onto the character and saves it through the ordinary store —
    /// whichever one it lives in — so the index fields follow exactly as they do for any other
    /// edit. Not the row that is open on screen: this control only ever appears on an "other" row,
    /// so a read-modify-write here can never race the session's own autosave.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task SaveVersionOf(string characterId)
    {
        if (await Store.ReadAsync(characterId) is not { } got) { _versionOfOpenId = null; return; }

        got.Sheet.Variant = _versionOfRoot.Length == 0
            ? null
            : new CharacterVariant(_versionOfRoot, _versionOfKind);

        var label = _characters.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Id == characterId)?.Label ?? "Unnamed character";
        await Store.RestoreAsync(characterId, label, got.Sheet, got.Mode);

        _versionOfOpenId = null;
        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>"Not a version" — clears the link without opening the chooser first.</summary>
    private async Task ClearVersionOf(string characterId)
    {
        _versionOfRoot = "";
        await SaveVersionOf(characterId);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What to call the character on screen.
    ///
    /// <para>An unnamed character says so rather than falling back to a blank — the same choice the
    /// review step's title and the banner's switcher both make, and for the same reason: a control
    /// with nothing in it reads as broken rather than as empty.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string OpenName =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Session.Sheet.Name) ? "Unnamed character" : Session.Sheet.Name;

    /// <summary>
    /// The one figure on this panel, and it is free only here.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The session is already holding this character, so no read is needed.</b> Every
    /// other row would cost a deserialize, a cost and a validate — see the panel's remarks — which
    /// is why they carry a time instead.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Shown only when the engine can give one</b>, the same rule the front door follows:
    /// a half-chosen Power is a question the engine refuses rather than guesses at, and the honest
    /// answer for that character is its name and no figure.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string Standing
    {
        get
        {
            if (!Session.HasSomethingToLose) return "Nothing chosen yet";
            if (CharacterSession.TryCost(() => Session.Spent) is not { } spent) return "";

            return Session.ShowBudget && Session.Budget > 0
                ? $"{spent} of {Session.Budget} Hero Points"
                : $"{spent} Hero Points spent";
        }
    }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        Accounts.Changed += Reload;
        Session.Changed += Redraw;

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The open block reads the session — its name and its spend — so a change to the character has
    /// to redraw this panel. The list itself is not re-read here: it changes when a character is
    /// saved, discarded or switched, and re-reading storage on every keystroke of the finishing
    /// step would be a read per letter typed. Same split the banner's switcher makes.
    /// </summary>
    private void Redraw() => StateHasChanged();

    /// <summary>
    /// Re-reads the list. Called on every change so the character marked open is the one that is.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Refresh()
    {
        // Any re-read clears the last refusal. Discarding a row to make room is the obvious way a
        // reader answers "your account is full", and a message that outlived the thing it was
        // about would then be telling them it did not work.
        _refusal = null;

        var listed = await Store.ListAsync();

        _characters = listed.Characters;
        _limit = listed.Limit;
        _full = listed.Limit is not null && listed.IsFull;
        _currentId = await Store.CurrentIdAsync();

        // Asked once for the whole panel. Null — not empty — for a visitor who is not signed in or
        // whose network is down, so no row claims a standing nobody could check.
        _memberships = _limit is null ? null : await Memberships.MineAsync();

        _others = [.. _characters.Where(c => c.Id != _currentId)];

        // Asked once for the whole panel too, and only where there is a list to group. A campaign
        // id on a row is not a name, and resolving one per row is the read-per-row this panel
        // refuses for the Hero Point figure. An account with no campaigns, and a visitor who is
        // not signed in, both answer an empty list here — which the headings handle as
        // "not here" rather than inventing a name. See `Roster.NameOf`.
        _names = ListsEveryCharacter
            ? new RosterNames(
                (await Campaigns.ListAsync()).ToDictionary(c => c.Id, c => c.Label, StringComparer.Ordinal),

                // The tiers come out of the rules the engine is already holding — no read, no
                // request, and no copy of a name that `data/rules/tiers.json` owns.
                Session.Rules.Tiers.ToDictionary(t => t.Id, t => t.Name, StringComparer.Ordinal))
            : RosterNames.None;

        // **A time earns its place only once there is something to tell apart.** Beside one other
        // character it is noise on a row that needs none; with several it is how a reader picks the
        // right one. Free either way — `UpdatedAt` is already in the index. What has changed is
        // that this is no longer sufficient on its own; see `ShowsTime`.
        _showTimes = _characters.Count >= 2;

        // Both kinds present, counted over what is actually listed rather than assumed from the
        // reader's own palette: the question is whether this list needs telling apart, not what
        // the person looking at it happens to be building.
        _showKinds = _others.Any(c => c.Kind == "hero") && _others.Any(c => c.Kind == "villain");

        // Over the whole account, like _memberships and _names above it and for the same reason
        // Arrange's own remarks give for VARIANT_ROOT_NOT_HELD: a family's root may be listed
        // under a different heading than one of its forms, so this has to see every character
        // this browser or account holds rather than one heading's worth.
        var families = CharacterVariants.Group(_characters.Select(CharacterVariants.FromSummary));
        _formNotes = await AlternateFormRosterNotes.BuildAsync(families, Forms, LoadSheetAsync);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The one load <see cref="AlternateFormRosterNotes"/> is handed — <see cref="Store"/>'s own
    /// by-id read, with the mode it also returns dropped: a family's findings do not care whether
    /// a member is a Hero or a Villain sheet, only what it paid and what tier it is built to.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task<CharacterSheet?> LoadSheetAsync(string id) => (await Store.ReadAsync(id))?.Sheet;

    /// <summary>Every family finding this account's engine check has about one member, if any.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<AlternateFormRosterNotes.Note> FamilyNotesFor(string id) =>
        _formNotes.ByMemberId.GetValueOrDefault(id, []);

    /// <summary>The shared Resolve pool to print on a family's root row, if this is one.</summary>
    private string? FamilyPoolFor(string id) => _formNotes.SharedResolveByRootId.GetValueOrDefault(id);

    private void Reload() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        await Refresh();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    private async Task Open(string id)
    {
        // The id travels with the sheet: this is the app saying which character is on screen,
        // which is the claim `CharacterSession.HeldId` exists to be able to make.
        if (await Store.OpenAsync(id) is { } opened) Session.Open(opened.Sheet, opened.Mode, id);

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Throws the character on screen away, and empties the sheet with it.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Order matters and is asserted.</b> Emptying the session raises its change event,
    /// which fires a save nobody awaits; clearing first races that write.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task DiscardOpen()
    {
        Session.StartAgain();
        await Store.ClearAsync();

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Throws away a character that is not the one on screen. Undoable, like everything here.
    ///
    /// <para>It is read back <em>before</em> the delete and handed to
    /// <see cref="DiscardedCharacter"/>, which is the only thing that could put it back: the
    /// session has never held that character and the store copy is what this is about to
    /// remove.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Read with <c>ReadAsync</c> and not <c>OpenAsync</c>.</b> The latter moves the
    /// current-character pointer on the way past, so reading a row in order to remember it would
    /// switch the app to the character being discarded — and the next autosave would write the
    /// sheet on screen over the id just restored.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Delete(string id)
    {
        // Remembered first: after the delete there is nothing left to read.
        if (await Store.ReadAsync(id) is { } going)
        {
            var label = _characters.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Id == id)?.Label ?? "";
            await Discarded.RememberAsync(id, label, going.Sheet, going.Mode);
        }
        else
        {
            // Nothing readable to offer back — and the stale offer from a previous discard must not
            // be left standing over a row this one could not keep.
            Discarded.Forget();
        }

        await Store.DeleteAsync(id);

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Keeps the character on screen and opens an empty one beside it.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It used to empty the slot instead, and that is the defect this closes.</b>
    /// <c>StartAgain</c> plus <c>ClearAsync</c> threw the character away — the owner imported one,
    /// pressed this, and lost it. Keeping it is
    /// <see cref="AccountCharacterStore.StartAnotherAsync"/>, which writes it down under its own id
    /// and moves the pointer before anything here empties the session.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>No undo, because nothing was destroyed and an undo here would duplicate.</b>
    /// <c>Undo</c> restores into the sheet without moving the current-character pointer, which by
    /// now is on the fresh slot — so undoing would write a second copy of the character just kept,
    /// and it would be listed twice. The kept character is a row above this button; that is the way
    /// back.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task StartNew()
    {
        if (await Keep() is false) return;

        Session.StartAgain(offerUndo: false);

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// An imported character goes into a slot of its own rather than over the one on screen.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It used to overwrite, which is half of what the owner reported.</b> The old call
    /// was <see cref="CharacterSession.ReplaceWithUndo"/>, whose whole purpose is the one-level
    /// undo that makes overwriting survivable — and it is the right call for the two things that
    /// really do replace the character on screen without moving the pointer, loading a sample and
    /// opening a recording. This no longer does: the character being left behind is written down
    /// under its own id first, so there is nothing to undo and an undo would only put a duplicate
    /// of it into the imported character's slot.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Imported((CharacterSheet Sheet, SheetMode Mode) character)
    {
        if (await Keep() is false) return;

        // The bare `NotifyChanged()` that used to follow this is inside `Open` now — it was the
        // single call site that had been patched for the silence, and patching one is what left
        // the other five wrong.
        // `Keep` has already moved the pointer to a fresh slot, so the imported character is
        // what that slot holds from here on — see `CharacterSession.HeldId`.
        Session.Open(character.Sheet, character.Mode, await Store.CurrentIdAsync());

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Writes the character on screen down under its own id and opens a fresh slot, reporting in
    /// words when it could not. False means nothing moved and the caller must leave the session
    /// exactly as it is.
    ///
    /// <para>Shared by both controls above because they are the same act with different things put
    /// into the new slot, and because a refusal must read the same either way — two copies of this
    /// would be two chances for one of them to forget to say anything.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task<bool> Keep()
    {
        var outcome = await Store.StartAnotherAsync(Session.Sheet, Session.Mode);

        if (outcome == StartAnotherOutcome.Started) return true;

        // Refreshed first, then the reason written over what that cleared: a refusal is about the
        // list as it stands after the attempt, and re-reading it is also how a full account whose
        // cap has since changed stops claiming to be full.
        await Refresh();

        _refusal = outcome switch
        {
            StartAnotherOutcome.NoRoom =>
                "Your account is full. Discard one to make room; the character you have is still saved.",

            // Kept, but no room could be confirmed — so the sentence says the character is safe,
            // which the one below it must not, because there it is not.
            StartAnotherOutcome.NotStarted =>
                "Your character is saved. Nothing new could be started just now — try again in a moment.",

            // Nothing was written and nothing can be, until the character behind the pointer is
            // read: what is on screen is not it, and keeping it here would put it over them. So
            // this sentence must not say "try again in a moment" the way the two around it do —
            // waiting changes nothing, and the way out is a read.
            StartAnotherOutcome.NothingRead =>
                "Your character could not be loaded into this browser, so what is on screen was not "
                + "saved and nothing new was started. Open one from the list above first.",

            _ => "Your character could not be saved just now, so nothing new was started. "
                 + "Try again in a moment.",
        };

        return false;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Accounts.Changed -= Reload;
        Session.Changed -= Redraw;
    }
}
